      "text": "We convert the positive integer $n$ to base $b$ by repeatedly dividing it by $b$ and writing the remainder, starting from $b$, then $b-1$, and so on until we reach zero. Therefore, $473_{10} = 1285_8$, so $\\boxed{1}$ is the first digit in base $8$ representation of $473_{10}$. To determine the first digit of the base \\(8\\) representation of the positive integer \\(473_{10}\\), we will follow the process of converting the decimal number to its base \\(8\\) equivalent step by step.\n\nWe start by repeatedly dividing \\(473\\) by \\(8\\) and recording the remainders, starting from the divisor, i.e., \\(8\\), and ending with the quotient.\n\n1. **First Division:**\n   \\[\n   \\frac{473}{8} = 59 \\quad \\text{remainder} \\quad 1\n   \\]\n   Since \\(473\\) divided by \\(8\\) gives a quotient of \\(59\\) with a remainder of \\(1\\), we write down \\(1\\) next to the remainder obtained from the previous division.\n\n2. **Second Division:**\n   \\[\n   59 \\div 8 = 7 \\quad \\text{remainder} \\quad 3\n   \\]\n   The quotient is now \\(7\\) and the remainder is \\(3\\), which will be recorded to the right of the first remainder.\n\n3. **Third Division:**\n   \\[\n   7 \\div 8 = 0 \\quad \\text{remainder} \\quad 7\n   \\]\n   The quotient is now \\(0\\) and the remainder is \\(7\\), which should be recorded to the right of the second remainder.\n\n4. **Final Division:**\n   A remainder of \\(0\\) indicates the end of the process. The quotient \\( \\frac{473}{8} = 59 \\) can be written directly after these remainders.\n\nThus, the base \\(8\\) representation of \\(473_{10}\\) is \\(1371_8\\).\n\nObserve that in base \\(8\\), each digit's position corresponds to powers of \\(8\\), and the digit represents the remainder upon division by \\(8\\):\n- The least significant digit (\\(\\text{units place}\\)) corresponds to \\(8^0\\),\n- The next digit (\\(\\text{tens place}\\)) corresponds to \\(8^1\\),\n- The next digit (\\(\\text{hundreds place}\\)) corresponds to \\(8^2\\), and so on.\n\nTo identify the first digit in base \\(8\\) representation, specifically \\(1371_8\\):\n1. The least significant digit \\(1\\) is multiplied by \\(8^0\\) because it occupies the units place.\n   \\[\n   1 \\cdot 8^0 = 1 \\cdot 1 = 1\n   \\]\n2. The next digit \\(3\\) is multiplied by \\(8^1\\) because it occupies the tens place.\n   \\[\n   3 \\cdot 8^1 = 3 \\cdot 8 = 24\n   \\]\n3. Proceed with the next digit \\(7\\) as it occupies the hundreds place.\n   \\[\n   7 \\cdot 8^2 = 7 \\cdot 64 = 448\n   \\]\n4. The last \\(1\\) is multiplied by \\(8^3\\) since it occupies the thousands place, though it is practically \\(0\\) for the ones place; hence, it simplifies to \\(1\\).\n   \\[\n   1 \\cdot 8^3 = 1 \\cdot 512 = 512\n   \\]\n\nNow, compile all values:\n\\[\n1371_8 = 1 \\cdot 8^3 + 24 \\cdot 8^2 + 448 \\cdot 8^1 + 512 \\cdot 8^0\n\\]\n\nHence, the first digit of the base \\(8\\) representation is derived from:\n\\[\n1371_8 = 1 \\cdot 8^3 + 24 \\cdot 8^2 + 448 \\cdot 8 + 512\n\\]\nThe first digit, which corresponds to the value of \\(8^0\\), is:\n\\[\n1 \\cdot 8^0 = 1\n\\]\n\nThus, the first digit (from left to right) in base \\(8\\) representation of \\(473_{10}\\) is \\(\\boxed{1}\\).",
